Text: REGULATORY REQUIREMENTS FOR AUTHORISED PERSONS ENGAGED IN REGULATED ACTIVITIES IN RELATION TO VIRTUAL ASSETS
Anti-Money Laundering and Countering Financing of Terrorism
Further, the purpose and scope of the FATF Guidance is to clarify and assist:
a)	national authorities in understanding and developing regulatory and supervisory responses to VA activities and VASPs with particular regard to the application of a RBA to their activities;

b)	in the supervision or monitoring of VASPs for AML/CFT purposes;

c)	in the licensing or registration of VASPs based on an applicable jurisdiction’s requirements, subject to effective systems for monitoring/supervision;

d)	in developing preventive measures including customer due diligence, recordkeeping, and suspicious transaction reporting, among others;

e)	in the implementation of sanctions and other enforcement measures, as well as international co-operation;

f)	in understanding risk indicators that should specifically be considered in a VA context, in relation to the obfuscation of transactions or limitations relating to a VASPs’ ability to identify customers; and

g)	the private sector seeking to engage in VA activities in understanding their AML/CFT obligations and how they can effectively comply with these requirements.

raw_model_output:
{
  "answer":"VASPs should apply FATF’s risk-based approach by identifying and assessing the ML/TF risks arising from their virtual-asset activities and taking effective action to mitigate those risks. [PID:888bc103-f2d4-4a46-a596-9b1ac120e6df]\n\nTo implement “effective action” in practice, a VASP should embed the RBA into its compliance culture, periodically perform risk-based assessments of its processes and activities, and have processes to identify, assess, monitor, manage and mitigate ML risks, applying enhanced measures where ML risk is higher. [PID:a068d4e0-2329-407f-8fa3-06bf38c0a3f5]\n\nEffective mitigation should include preventive controls such as: (i) customer risk assessment and robust CDD for each client prior to transacting (including appropriate non-face-to-face identity verification methods), (ii) effective transaction monitoring/“know your transaction” measures to determine the origin and destination of virtual assets and detect possible ML/TF and sanctions breaches (potentially using monitoring algorithms/AI), (iii) internal risk “indicators” and processes to manage triggered indicators (e.g., use of mixers/tumblers), (iv) governance, systems and controls (including a tailored Virtual Asset Compliance Policy, periodic compliance monitoring program, and an appropriately senior and independent MLRO), (v) suspicious activity reporting readiness (including connectivity with the FIU), and (vi) recordkeeping arrangements that ensure the VASP and regulator can access relevant information (including where DLT is used for records). [PID:a068d4e0-2329-407f-8fa3-06bf38c0a3f5]\n\nRisk identification and mitigation should reflect key VA-specific risk factors FATF highlights (e.g., (pseudo-)anonymity and non-face-to-face relationships, cross-border reach, complex multi-entity infrastructures, and exposure to jurisdictions with inadequate ML/TF controls). [PID:eb50c0be-29f7-4df2-8022-3c40adf1edb5]",
