  "answer": "For a Petroleum Reporting Entity seeking admission to the FSRA Official List, Chapter 12 does not replace other disclosure regimes; it adds petroleum-specific disclosure requirements on top of those that already apply to (i) applicants for admission/listed or reporting entities and (ii) persons making (or intending to make) an offer of securities. [PID:f7a0a5d1-932b-45d4-a093-f56b7e7100ce]\n\nFSRA guidance indicates Chapter 12 operates closely with other parts of the Rules—including Chapter 2 (Listing Rules), Chapter 4 (Offers of Securities), Chapter 7 (Market Disclosure), and Chapter 10 (Financial reports/auditing)—so that if a disclosure is required under Rule 7.2.1 about petroleum activities, the disclosure must comply with both Rule 7.2.1 and the applicable Chapter 12 requirements. [PID:3863e612-3064-4d97-8e7f-cf44caf0b62e]\n\nIn an admission/offer context, overlapping obligations can therefore apply concurrently (e.g., a prospectus must meet Chapter 4/Appendix 1 content requirements and also include the Chapter 12 petroleum-resource disclosures). [PID:3863e612-3064-4d97-8e7f-cf44caf0b62e]\n\nFSRA also indicates it expects these inter-related rules to be applied to entities undertaking the full framework when seeking listing/trading (including complying with the ongoing disclosure rules in their entirety, including Chapter 11). [PID:97b443fc-5c1e-4bfd-a56f-d4def431592a]\n\nOn timing/trigger points, FSRA’s view is that the obligation to disclose material information about petroleum activities under Rule 7.2.1 and Chapter 12 will generally be triggered only when the entity possesses all the information required to be included under Chapter 12 for that petroleum-activity disclosure; earlier information may be viewed as insufficiently definite, and if it remains confidential, FSRA may consider it not immediately disclosable under Rule 7.2.2. [PID:2cb6aab3-73ce-4c82-9801-09ae2249abde] [PID:209d701c-4166-4d1b-a133-4819fe44c995]",
